  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1240 AM EDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nUpper level ridging currently pinches up the SE CONUS with \nsurface high pressure centered just off of the Mid- Atlantic. One \nlast day of area-wide dry weather and abnormally hot temperatures \ntoday, as a closed low within the pattern eventually phases with \nthe trough with its eastward progression. A frontal system \ncurrently stretching north into the TX and OK panhandles, will be \nknocking on our doorstep as we welcome the first weekend of \nOctober. With the surface high pressure situated to the east, \nsoutherly flow will bring a marked return of higher dew points \nthis afternoon compared to yesterday.\n\nMoisture surrounds the area during the early morning hours tomorrow \nto our west and south. The frontal system is forecast to enter the \nMississippi Valley around that time. Although tomorrow starts out \ndry, precipitation chances increase the further we get into the day. \nThe frontal system does cross the area sometime late tomorrow into \nearly Saturday, however, it will become stationary just to our south \nand not totally clear the area until possibly later Sunday. What \nthis means will be periods of wet weather for the entire area this \nweekend. So far, a marginal risk for flash flooding is in place. \nWith the highest FFG across our southern areas, some places could \nhave the capacity to absorb a lot of the rainfall not bringing much \nof a threat unless training occurs with consecutive heavy \nshowers/storms. Right now, Sunday has the heaviest rainfall \naccumulation as the low center moves overhead as it begins to push \nout. Forecast PWATs at this time do show values nearing or going \njust above 2 inches which would be conducive for heavy rain makers. \nAs far as any storms, there isn't a severe threat, but there could \nbe embedded storms. Instability overall will be lacking. The \ngreatest threat this weekend will be heavy rain with the possibility \nof localized flash flooding. Grand total QPF amounts, though being \nfine tuned with each forecast, show the entire forecast area seeing \nat least one inch of rain. Some areas could see over two, possibly \nup to 3 inches when everything is said and done. Due to overall \nuncertainty of amounts still, high FFG, etc. have elected to \ncontinue with low probability of hazardous weather for Friday onward.\n\nRain clears the region as we enter a new work week next week. A \nsecondary front will send everything out of here replacing it with \ndry weather, northerly flow, and temperatures typically recorded in \nearly October. Surface high pressure sinks in from the north and \nfrontal systems until the end of the period are forecast to remain \nwell to our south and to the north, for now.",
